// PlanB64: offline IPv6 FIB compiler and longest-prefix lookup tool.
//
// Longest-prefix matching over prefixes of length at most 64 is reduced to
// predecessor search over disjoint intervals of the 64-bit upper-address key
// space.  `build` compiles a FIB into the minimal change-point representation
// of the function  key -> (next hop, prefix length);  `lookup` answers trace
// queries with a binary search over the compiled rows.
//
// Compilation idea.  A prefix of length L covers the aligned dyadic key range
// [start, start + 2^(64-L)).  Dyadic ranges are laminar: two of them either
// nest or are disjoint.  Hence at any candidate change point the covering
// prefixes form a chain ordered by length, so a single stack sweep over the
// sorted candidate boundaries yields the winning route of every elementary
// interval in O(n log n).  Candidate boundaries are exactly 0 plus every
// prefix start plus every prefix end below 2^64, which bounds the row count by
// 2*records + 1.

